<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neman</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nemunas, Niemen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neman, or Nemunas, is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> about 900 kilometres long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> to the Curonian Lagoon on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Its lower course forms the border between Lithuania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the frontier and battleground between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and in 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> met on a raft on the river at Tilsit.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neman.jpg" alt="Neman" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neman_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Neman, called Nemunas in Lithuanian and Niemen in Polish, is the largest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and the third largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, and is navigable for most of its length. It begins as two small headwaters that merge about 15 km south-west of Uzda, some 55 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>. Of its course, 436 km lie in Belarus and 359 km in Lithuania, and 116 km form the border between Lithuania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kaliningrad oblast. It has some 105 first-class tributaries, the largest being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neris">Neris</a>, the Shchara and the Šešupė, and a basin of 98,200 km2.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> the river makes loops that follow an underlying tectonic fault, not conventional meanders; near Prienai one loop, 17 km long, comes within 1.2 km of closing. Their castles served as the first line of defence against the Teutonic knights. At its delta the river splits into branches and canals, of which the main ones are the Atmata, Pakalnė, Skirvytė and Gilija, and it provides the main water inflow of the Curonian Lagoon, keeping the water almost fresh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ptolemy called the river Chronos, though some think that name was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pregolya">Pregolya</a>. It gave its name to the Neman Culture, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> archaeological subculture. In German the part flowing through historic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> has been called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> at least since about 1250, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> built Memelburg castle and the town of Memel at the mouth of the Curonian Lagoon, naming it after the river's indigenous name. The border between the State of the Teutonic Order and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, fixed in 1422 by the Treaty of Lake Melno, remained stable for centuries.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> signed the Treaty of Tilsit on a raft in the river in 1807, and Napoleon's crossing at the start of the 1812 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> is described in War and Peace and mentioned in Pan Tadeusz. In 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> made the river the border separating the Memel Territory from German East Prussia. The first stanza of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>, written in 1841, names it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s eastern boundary. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> call the Nemunas the father of rivers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river is used for fishing, hydropower, water supply, industry, agriculture, recreation and water transport. Its largest cities are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, Alytus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and Sovetsk in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a>. A dam above Kaunas, built in 1959, formed the Kaunas Reservoir, the largest lake of its kind in Lithuania, and the Augustów Canal, built in the 19th century, connects the Neman with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>. Nemunas Loops Regional Park and Nemunas Delta Regional Park were both founded in 1992.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lithuanian tribes in the forests between the <b>Neman</b> and the Neris remained pagan long after their neighbours converted, organised under dukes whose number is suggested by a treaty of 1219 with Galicia–Volhynia that names twenty-one of them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On an islet of the <b>Neman</b> River, Vytautas the Great, ruler of Lithuania, and Grand Master Konrad von Jungingen signed the Treaty of Salynas, promising Samogitia to the Teutonic Knights for the third time.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-12">12 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Versailles separated the land north of the <b>Neman</b>, including the port of Memel (Klaipėda), from Germany, placing it under the Allies in 1920 with a French administration.</blockquote><a href="/state/klaipeda-region">Klaipėda Region</a></li><li><blockquote>Nadruvia lay in the north-east of Prussia, south of the <b>Neman</b>, in what is now the Kaliningrad region.</blockquote><a href="/state/nadruvians">Nadruvians</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scalovians, or Skalvians, lived on both banks of the lower <b>Neman</b> in what is now the Kaliningrad region of Russia and western Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/scalovians">Scalovians</a></li></ul></section>
